1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,929
A group of folk who worked the evening shift often passed the club room without stopping.

2
00:00:04,929 --> 00:00:11,053
Obviously they had something to say, and neither Lin nor Maya had asked them.

3
00:00:11,053 --> 00:00:20,642
Maya walked along the queue with her notebook, raised her hand to greet each worker, and kept a diary of every remark she heard.

4
00:00:20,642 --> 00:00:26,121
She was willing to evaluate the evidence without defending her own idea.

5
00:00:26,121 --> 00:00:32,256
“Regarding the map,” one worker said, “you are asking the wrong people.

6
00:00:32,256 --> 00:00:42,235
Your notes record where the doors are, but not who cannot reach them.” The two problems were equally serious.

7
00:00:42,235 --> 00:00:51,038
On Thursday a drunken man in a worn coat leaned sideways against the railing and exclaimed that the east gate had no ramp at all.

8
00:00:51,038 --> 00:00:57,302
He was not resistant to help; he was simply accustomed to being asked nothing.

9
00:00:57,302 --> 00:01:08,866
When Maya said goodbye, he added, aloud, that the queue needed a suitable ramp and that the gossip in the line was more informative than any official notice.

10
00:01:08,866 --> 00:01:11,161
She wrote it down.

11
00:01:11,161 --> 00:01:20,160
Anything else, she decided, would be guesswork, and anyway a survey that ignores a queue is not a survey.

12
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:26,454
The form asked for age and sex before it asked whether the door opened.

13
00:01:26,454 --> 00:01:32,943
The path to the east gate ran down a steep slope with a geological fault under the paving.

14
00:01:32,943 --> 00:01:37,472
It was picturesque in photographs and useless in rain.

15
00:01:37,472 --> 00:01:44,261
On ceremonial days the gate opened for guests; on ordinary days it awaited a guard.

16
00:01:44,261 --> 00:02:00,119
“Liberty,” Maya wrote in her diary, “is a door that opens without a ceremony.” Professor Zhao read the sentence and said that an original observation is worth more than a rate with three decimal places.

17
00:02:00,119 --> 00:02:03,159
Lin was inclined to be forgiving.

18
00:02:03,159 --> 00:02:11,132
The mistake was divisive only if they hid it, and a secretive report would look creditable while proving nothing.

19
00:02:11,132 --> 00:02:15,477
With modesty, they listed what they did not know.

20
00:02:15,477 --> 00:02:34,259
The submissive tone of the old notice, the stuffy room where it was written, and the joyful assumption that everything worked — all of it pointed onward to one eventuality: someone would be left outside, and their promise of change would never reach that person.

21
00:02:34,259 --> 00:02:39,669
Maya wiped the whiteboard and turned it outwards so the corridor could read it.

22
00:02:39,669 --> 00:02:49,353
She was fond of the small map, but she was impatient with its silence, and she had thought about the missing voice until midnight.

23
00:02:49,353 --> 00:02:57,971
The work was not rewarding in the way a score is rewarding; it was rewarding because a stranger could now find the gate.

24
00:02:57,971 --> 00:03:01,981
Lin felt contented for the first time that month.

25
00:03:01,981 --> 00:03:09,003
“Otherwise,” Maya said, “we are only decorating a wall.”
